# Gate review r2 — M-next D11, the fenced writer after the link/close round (read-only)

Reviewed the whole uncommitted diff in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d11` (branch `ucf-d11-writer`, on 441b2b4c): `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer.cljc` (new, 535 lines), `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` (new, 1125 lines, 25 deftests), `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` (57+/11− — byte-identical to the diff my r1 passed, so it carries r1's READY unchanged). Read against the r2 sign-off brief, the architect's r1/r2 sign-offs, the link-cursor ruling, the D5 close ruling, UCF 7.7.5/7.7.8, and the front/admission/ledger/module code the writer composes with. Note: an architect r3 sign-off exists (collab/1791280000000, completed 11:48 GMT, after the brief was written); I verified its finding independently rather than adopting it — it checks out, and it is the same defect I found.

## Verdict: **NOT READY**

One contract defect must change before landing (finding 1, below — the terminal-outcome half of the r2 sign-off's item 2, still unapplied, and independently the r3 architect's CHANGES), plus the test rows the architect's r2 CHANGES explicitly demanded that are still absent (findings 2–4), plus the standing landing condition that no lane run exists on the final tree (finding 5). Everything else I attacked held: the four r2 repairs are otherwise correctly in, the root-path/OOM repair is correct and the empty-path sweep is clean, close ordering holds per stream per machine, and all eight of my r1 attack points still pass on the final files.

## The attack list, point by point

**1. The two new obligations, end to end — one hole.**

*Link writes.* Live minting is fully gone: no `stream/cursor`, no `apply-link-cursor` call anywhere in writer.cljc (grep confirms the only cursor reference is the emittability check at writer.cljc:248). A cursorless `:link-request` — enrolled or bare — fails `write-emittable?` (writer.cljc:247–248) and is skipped with no send, no observation, id retained structurally (enrolled sends never mutate the machine; writer.cljc:378–381). `link-requests-assigned-and-emitted-test` (test:962–975) and `link-cursor-ruling-test` (test:1046–1060) pin zero sends and no `:cursor` key; the recorded cursor is installed by the test through the real `engine/apply-link-cursor` (test:983, 1062) — the driver-side observation stays D12's, as ruled. With the cursor installed, emit assigns (from the root's one counter — link entries are writes, writer.cljc:79–84), sends through the fence with `:yin.k/value` = the entry's `:envelope` (writer.cljc:149–151), and an authenticated `:committed` discharges through `apply-link-sent` (writer.cljc:418–419 → engine.cljc:1626–1637), which correctly demands the cursor (`Link request sent before its cursor`) and refuses a second transition — the test authors the admission reply (disclosed, test:993–995) and the five match fields still run. The bare path selects the actual handle `module/link-request-resource` (writer.cljc:366–368; the r2 bug was `(:stream-id entry)`) and appends the raw envelope, matching `module/append-link-request`'s payload (module.cljc:497). Target resolution for class lookup uses the same resource (writer.cljc:109–111). **The hole:** `apply-effect`'s link arm (writer.cljc:269–272) transitions on `:ok` and retains the entry on **every** other outcome — `:full` *and* terminal. That is not the link protocol: `module/append-link-request` retains on `:full` but **throws** on terminal outcomes (module.cljc:498–504), and in the ungated machine that throw surfaces as the require's error — the parked program learns. Under the writer, a closed or refused link-request stream is re-appended on every emit, forever; the entry never wakes and never errors. Worse on the recorded side: `settle` (writer.cljc:416–423) reports `:discharged` unconditionally for `:committed`/`:replayed`, so a terminal recorded `:yin.k/effect-result` leaves the entry pending (apply-effect no-ops) while drain reports it discharged — contradicting drain's own contract ("a record that retains changes nothing", writer.cljc:518–520) — and the next emit resends, replaying the admission in a loop. Every other apply in this design gives terminal outcomes the failure disposition (`apply-put` engine.cljc:1922–1931, `apply-ffi-outcome` engine.cljc:2000–2018: terminal wakes under its own status); the link arm is the sole divergence. And `bare-link-path-test`'s second block (test:1086–1091) **pins the wrong behavior** ("terminal outcome retains and reports"). This is also exactly the r3 architect's CHANGES; the fix round is already owed. Required: a shared link-outcome apply preserving the terminal failure disposition, used by both the bare arm and `settle`, and the pinned assertion replaced with regressions for terminal failure, `:full` retention, successful transition, and no subsequent retry of a terminally failed write.

*Close ordering.* The interleave is right: each loop iteration resolves the first resolvable close before any write (writer.cljc:309–345), `close-resolvable?` (writer.cljc:229–239) blocks a close behind any same-stream waiting put with a lower issue — including a put **awaiting its admission outcome**, since sent enrolled writes stay in the wait set until discharge — and `write-emittable?` (writer.cljc:250–255) blocks a put behind a same-stream close with a lower issue. `close-ordering-test` (test:1014–1043) pins restored-put → close → later-put at the root, through the real park with real issue stamps, discharge of the first put, then diagnostic + resolution + the second send. Close records always carry `:yin.k/issue` (engine.cljc:750–754), program puts always get one under the gate (engine.cljc:2608–2612), so the comparisons are safe; readers deliberately don't block closes (handle-close's own contract, engine.cljc:740–746). An id-less enrolled put (exhausted counter) blocks its close forever — correct fail-safe. Per-machine scoping is the only coherent reading, since issue numbers are machine-local counters (engine.cljc:549–554); see finding 7. The three class arms: `:at-least-once` calls `close!` on the machine's own handle **before** `apply-close` (writer.cljc:321–328 — the r2 repair, verified; memory-log and ringbuffer both answer `:closed` appends after `close!`, memory_log.cljc:157–176, ringbuffer.cljc:120–123), `close-undeclared-test` (test:1113–1125) pins nil class → `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the identity with the close retained and no stream effect (writer.cljc:331–333), the enrolled close resolves with the `:enrolled-close` diagnostic and is never closed (writer.cljc:336–345), and `:fail-stop` ends the run with children stamped (writer.cljc:317–318). Missing rows: findings 2–4.

**2. r1's eight attack points — all still hold.** Assign-before-send atomicity, the five-key envelope and tagged request id, retention across the four cases, the five match fields each refusing alone, `:intent-conflict` aftermath with quarantine surviving release, the protection classes, the carried engine obligations, and the unknown-effect cuts: the code for all of these is unchanged from the diff r1 passed (verified by re-reading, not by diff-of-diff trust), and their tests are intact (test:297–637, 642–719, 725–802, 861–955). The engine hunks (`refuse-unminted!` at engine.cljc:698–706 guarding `apply-next`:1949 and `apply-observation`; `ffi-request-slot` and `apply-ffi-outcome` at engine.cljc:1971–2018) are the same bytes r1 approved.

**3. The assoc-in repair and the empty-path sweep — correct and clean.** Both close arms and `apply-effect` use `(if (seq path) (assoc-in m path applied) (engine/apply-fn m ...))` (writer.cljc:277, 322–328, 336–342): root closes apply directly to `m` — `(assoc-in m [] x)` would have bolted a nil-keyed entry on, the OOM defect — and child closes thread through `assoc-in` on a non-empty path. `close-ordering-test` exercises the root leg through a real park; sweep of every other `assoc-in` in writer.cljc: `put-entry` conjoins `[:wait-set i]` (writer.cljc:133), `assign` uses a literal non-empty path (writer.cljc:200), `ended` conjoins `:yin.k/gate` (writer.cljc:214–215) — none can construct an empty path. The test file's one `assoc-in` (test:752) is non-empty. No other hazard exists.

**4. The one-word hardening — still absent, accepted for D11, re-stated.** Both conds fall through to `:else` = enrolled for writes (writer.cljc:377–382) and closes (writer.cljc:335–345); a class value outside the three is silently fenced/resolved rather than refused. Unreachable through the sanctioned path (D10's lower accepts exactly the three classes), and two architect signoffs have not required it — I accept its absence for D11 and re-state it as a one-word-each landing condition for the first slice that hand-builds a custody map (D13/D15 wiring).

**5. Portability and style.** No reader conditionals in writer.cljc; the test's one conditional (test:43) carries all three host branches; children and closes walked in `sort-by str` order (writer.cljc:75); no float literals; `contains?` for the cursor check. The lanes and lint are addressed in finding 5.

## Numbered findings

1. **MUST CHANGE — terminal link append outcomes are retained and retried forever instead of failing** (writer.cljc:269–272; the unapplied terminal half of the architect r2's item 2, and the r3 architect's CHANGES, independently confirmed). Bare path: a closed/refused link-request stream is re-appended every emit, the entry never wakes, the parked require never learns — the ungated protocol throws and surfaces the error (module.cljc:498–504). Recorded path: `settle` (writer.cljc:416–423) reports `:discharged` for a terminal `:yin.k/effect-result` while the entry stays pending — a discharged report for a retained write — and re-emission loops admission replays. `bare-link-path-test` test:1086–1091 pins the divergence as if it were the contract. Fix with a shared link-outcome apply carrying the terminal failure disposition (the pattern `apply-put`/`apply-ffi-outcome` already set), used for bare and recorded outcomes, plus the four regressions (terminal failure, `:full` retention, successful transition, no retry after terminal failure).
2. **Required pin (r2 CHANGES 3, "including an install child") — no child close regression exists.** All three close tests are root-only (test:1014–1043, 1094–1110, 1113–1125). The child close legs (writer.cljc:322–328, 336–342) are the exact `(assoc-in m path …)` shape of the repaired OOM defect and are unpinned; a child restored-put → close → later-put row is also what the r1 obligation asked ("including children"). Add it with finding 1's fix round.
3. **Required pin (r2 CHANGES 4, "alongside the three declared classes") — no `:fail-stop` close regression.** The arm at writer.cljc:317–318 (end the run, children stamped) is untested.
4. **Required pin (r2 CHANGES 2) — no `:full` case on the bare link path.** `ok` and `closed` are pinned (test:1083–1091); `:full` retention is only implied by the code.
5. **Landing condition (evidence): no lane or lint record on the final tree.** The last recorded runs predate the r2-CHANGES round — writer.cljc was rewritten and six tests added since (the orchestrator log's last entry is D10's landing). Before commit: full JVM/Node/Dart run and kondo/cljstyle over the three files, recorded. Nothing in the portability audit predicts a host failure, but this diff has moved twice since any suite ran.
6. **Accepted scope boundary worth one UCF sentence — cross-machine close/write ordering is not enforced.** `close-resolvable?`/`write-emittable?` compare issues within one machine only; issue numbers are machine-local (engine.cljc:549–554), so per-stream order is undefined across root/child and the same-machine scoping is the only coherent reading. Record it so D12/D13 don't assume cross-machine ordering where a root close and a child put share a target.
7. **Benign, noted for completeness — FFI requests never block closes.** FFI request entries carry no `:yin.k/issue`, so `write-emittable?`'s close gate skips them; unreachable in practice because the call-in stream is a private resource (ffi.cljc:35–37, module.cljc:448–452) no program close can name.
8. **Cosmetic.** test:1082 uses the fully-qualified `yin.vm.module/link-request-resource` against the file's own `module` alias; the `(when append-diagnostic! …)` guard at writer.cljc:343 is dead (the 2-arity default is a fn, always truthy); stray blank lines after a few test signatures (`ring`, `parked-put`, `counting-handle`). None blocking.

**What must change to land: finding 1 (code + its regressions), findings 2–4 (the architect-demanded rows), finding 5 (the recorded lanes). Findings 6–8 are notes. The engine.cljc hunks and everything carried from r1 may land as they stand.**
